{% block article_body %}
<section aria-labelledby="scope"><h2 id="scope">What the server wires up</h2>
<p>Start with the boundary, because it decides what can be built today. In {{ wireframe_version }}, a connection served by <code>WireframeServer</code> answers each routed request with one reply, split across frames only by fragmentation. The types on this page, which are <code>Response</code>, <code>PushQueues</code>, <code>ConnectionActor</code>, and <code>SessionRegistry</code>, ship, are tested, and work together. <code>WireframeServer</code> does not drive them, so adopting one means writing the glue.</p>
{% call ui.callout('fact', 'Building blocks, not yet a server feature') %}<p>Server-initiated pushes and multi-frame replies on a <code>WireframeServer</code> connection need a connection loop that runs the actor. That integration is upstream roadmap work, tracked for <a href="https://github.com/leynos/wireframe/issues/874">the actor and streaming responses</a> and for <a href="https://github.com/leynos/wireframe/issues/875">push queues and the session registry</a>. <a href="{{ source_base }}/docs/users-guide.md#building-blocks-not-yet-wired-into-the-server">The users&rsquo; guide</a> says how far each part goes. The client side is further along: <a href="/wireframe/guides/client/#streams"><code>call_streaming</code></a> already reads a multi-frame reply from any server that sends one.</p>{% endcall %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="response"><h2 id="response">Responses that stream</h2>
<p><code>Response</code> describes a reply of any shape: <code>Single</code> frame, a <code>Vec</code> of frames, a <code>Stream</code> of fallible frames, a <code>MultiPacket</code> channel, or <code>Empty</code>. <code>into_stream</code> turns any of them into one boxed stream, which is the form the connection actor consumes.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/streaming.rs', 'stream', title='streaming.rs · a stream of frames') }}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="multi-packet"><h2 id="multi-packet">Back-pressure through a channel</h2>
<p><code>Response::with_channel</code> returns a sender and a <code>MultiPacket</code> response around the matching receiver. A producer task sends frames whenever it has them. Each <code>send</code> waits while the channel is full, so a slow consumer slows the producer instead of growing a buffer. Dropping the last sender ends the response.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/streaming.rs', 'multi-packet', title='streaming.rs · a channel-backed reply') }}
<p>A protocol marks the end of such a stream with a terminator frame: the connection actor takes it from the <code>stream_end_frame</code> protocol hook, and the client recognizes it through <code>Packet::is_stream_terminator</code>.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="push"><h2 id="push">Priority push queues</h2>
<p><code>PushQueues</code> holds frames a connection should send without being asked: notifications, heartbeats, broadcast traffic. The builder sets a high- and a low-priority capacity, an optional rate limit, and an optional dead-letter queue. The rate limit defaults to 100 pushes a second, accepts up to 10,000, and is shared by both queues; <code>unlimited()</code> removes it.</p>
<p>The builder returns the queues and a cloneable <code>PushHandle</code>. Background tasks push through the handle: <code>push_high_priority</code> and <code>push_low_priority</code> wait for the rate limiter and then for room, while <code>try_push</code> skips the limiter and applies a <code>PushPolicy</code> instead of waiting, optionally warning and forwarding the frame to the dead-letter queue. <code>DropIfFull</code> without a dead-letter queue loses frames silently.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="actor"><h2 id="actor">The connection actor</h2>
<p><code>ConnectionActor</code> drains a connection&rsquo;s push queues and its current response into one ordered output, polling a cancellation token so it stops promptly on shutdown. High-priority frames go first, but <code>FairnessConfig</code> lets a low-priority frame through after at most eight high-priority ones by default, optionally after a time slice as well, so a busy high queue cannot starve the low one.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/streaming.rs', 'actor', title='streaming.rs · driving the actor') }}
<p><code>run</code> collects frames into a <code>Vec</code> the caller supplies, and does not write to a socket. The actor also runs the <code>WireframeProtocol</code> hooks installed with <code>with_protocol</code>. On a <code>WireframeServer</code> connection, which has no actor, only <code>before_send</code> runs; the others have no effect there.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="sessions"><h2 id="sessions">Find a connection later</h2>
<p><code>SessionRegistry</code> maps a <code>ConnectionId</code> to a connection&rsquo;s <code>PushHandle</code> so that other tasks can reach it. It holds each handle weakly: when the connection&rsquo;s queues are dropped, the entry no longer resolves, and stale entries are pruned on lookup or by <code>prune()</code>. It is not populated automatically: it tracks the handles the application inserts.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/streaming.rs', 'registry', title='streaming.rs · a weak registry') }}
<p><code>SessionRegistry::default()</code> requires the frame type to implement <code>Default</code>. <code>Envelope</code> does not, so in {{ wireframe_version }} a registry of envelope handles needs a frame type that does, or a wrapper.</p>
</section>
{% endblock %}
